        "# Exercise\n",
        "\n",
        "## Exercise 1.\n",
        "The goal here is to construct a clean dataset for cross-sectional stock return prediction. We keep observations from January 2000 onward, select a limited set of influential predictors, document missing-value handling, and prepare a feature matrix with characteristic - macro interactions and industry dummies."
      ],
      "id": "32d9483d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import tidyfinance as tf\n",
        "from plotnine import *\n",
        "from mizani.formatters import percent_format"
      ],
      "id": "2deebb24",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 1a: Variable selection and sample restriction\n",
        "First, we remove observations before year 2000, then we select ten stock-level characteristics based on the variable-importance results in @Gu.2020, especially Figure 5. The selected variables cover price trends (`characteristic_mom1m`, `characteristic_mom12m`), size and liquidity (`characteristic_mvel1`, `characteristic_turn`), risk and volatility (`characteristic_beta`, `characteristic_retvol`), and valuation/fundamentals (`characteristic_bm`, `characteristic_ep`, `characteristic_roaq`, `characteristic_invest`).\n",
        "\n",
        "For the macroeconomic state variables, we follow @Gu.2020 Table 4 and include aggregate book-to-market (`macro_bm`), the Treasury bill rate (`macro_tbl`), the default yield spread (`macro_dfy`), and the term spread (`macro_tms`). These variables capture aggregate valuation, interest rate conditions, credit risk, and the slope of the yield curve."
      ],
      "id": "aaba5fcd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "cache": false
      },
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "df = pd.read_parquet(\"data-python/stock_characteristics_monthly.parquet\")\n",
        "df.head()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Keep observations from January 2000 onward\n",
        "df[\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(df[\"date\"])\n",
        "df = df.loc[df[\"date\"] >= \"2000-01-01\"].copy()"
      ],
      "id": "1e1d5aa2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "stock_chars = [\n",
        "    \"characteristic_mom1m\",    # short-term reversal (price trend)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_mom12m\",   # 12-month momentum (price trend)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_mvel1\",    # size (liquidity)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_turn\",     # share turnover (liquidity)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_beta\",     # market beta (risk)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_retvol\",   # return volatility (risk)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_bm\",       # book-to-market (value)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_ep\",       # earnings-to-price (value)\n",
        "    \"characteristic_roaq\",     # profitability\n",
        "    \"characteristic_invest\",   # investment growth\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "macro_vars = [\n",
        "    \"macro_bm\",    # aggregate book-to-market\n",
        "    \"macro_tbl\",   # T-bill rate\n",
        "    \"macro_dfy\",   # default yield spread\n",
        "    \"macro_tms\",   # term spread\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "base_cols = [\"permno\", \"date\", \"ret_excess\", \"mktcap_lag\", \"sic2\"]\n",
        "df = df[base_cols + stock_chars + macro_vars].copy()"
      ],
      "id": "8f125972",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 1b: Summary Statistics and Missing Values\n",
        "Table 1 reports summary statistics for the selected predictors. Stock characteristics are approximately centered around zero and bounded close to $[-1,1]$, while macro variables vary at the monthly aggregate level. "
      ],
      "id": "7f46837c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "summary_table = (\n",
        "    df[stock_chars + macro_vars]\n",
        "    .describe()\n",
        "    .T[[\"mean\", \"std\", \"min\", \"25%\", \"50%\", \"75%\", \"max\"]]\n",
        "    .round(3)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "summary_table"
      ],
      "id": "dd8768ae",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We next document missing values before applying the imputation procedure."
      ],
      "id": "8c21f473"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "missing_table = (\n",
        "    df[stock_chars + macro_vars]\n",
        "    .isna()\n",
        "    .sum()\n",
        "    .rename(\"n_missing\")\n",
        "    .to_frame()\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "missing_table"
      ],
      "id": "f4162d3e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Missing stock characteristics are imputed using the monthly cross-sectional median. This preserves the number of stock-month observations while using only contemporaneous information from the same month. The median is preferred to the mean because it is less sensitive to extreme firm-level observations, which are common in financial data.\n",
        "\n",
        "Macroeconomic variables are common to all firms in a given month and vary only over time. Missing macro values are therefore filled forward and backward to preserve the time series of aggregate state variables. Finally, observations with missing excess returns or lagged market capitalization are dropped because `ret_excess` is the prediction target and `mktcap_lag` is required to construct value-weighted portfolio returns."
      ],
      "id": "f1043e34"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# impute stock characteristics with monthly cross-sectional medians\n",
        "for col in stock_chars:\n",
        "    df[col] = df.groupby(\"date\")[col].transform(lambda x: x.fillna(x.median()))\n",
        "\n",
        "# fill macro variables over time\n",
        "df[macro_vars] = df[macro_vars].ffill().bfill()\n",
        "\n",
        "# frop rows missing target or portfolio weight variable\n",
        "df = df.dropna(subset=[\"ret_excess\", \"mktcap_lag\"]).copy()"
      ],
      "id": "895ec44c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "post_missing = (\n",
        "    df[stock_chars + macro_vars + [\"ret_excess\", \"mktcap_lag\"]]\n",
        "    .isna()\n",
        "    .sum()\n",
        "    .rename(\"n_missing\")\n",
        "    .to_frame()\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "post_missing"
      ],
      "id": "adacb68a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We recode `sic2` into the 10 major SIC divisions to retain broad industry information while keeping the feature space manageable. This reduces the number of industry dummy variables and lowers the risk that sparse industry categories create noisy predictors in the machine learning models."
      ],
      "id": "ec3d4bb6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def recode_sic(sic):\n",
        "    if pd.isna(sic):\n",
        "        return \"Other\"\n",
        "    sic = int(sic)\n",
        "    if sic < 10:\n",
        "        return \"Agriculture\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 15:\n",
        "        return \"Mining\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 18:\n",
        "        return \"Construction\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 40:\n",
        "        return \"Manufacturing\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 50:\n",
        "        return \"Transportation\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 52:\n",
        "        return \"Wholesale\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 60:\n",
        "        return \"Retail\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 70:\n",
        "        return \"Finance\"\n",
        "    elif sic < 90:\n",
        "        return \"Services\"\n",
        "    else:\n",
        "        return \"Public Admin\"\n",
        "\n",
        "df[\"sic_division\"] = df[\"sic2\"].apply(recode_sic)"
      ],
      "id": "913c3f33",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "industry_counts = (\n",
        "    df[\"sic_division\"]\n",
        "    .value_counts()\n",
        "    .rename_axis(\"sic_division\")\n",
        "    .reset_index(name=\"n_obs\")\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "industry_counts"
      ],
      "id": "a4425d23",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Figure 1 plots the monthly cross-sectional mean excess return. The series illustrates that average realized excess returns vary substantially over time, highlighting the noisy nature of stock return prediction."
      ],
